`PyReadwriteArray1<'py, T>` (and any `Bound`/borrow-checked PyO3 numpy type) is generic over the GIL-bound lifetime `'py`, which only lives for the duration of the call — it can never satisfy `'static`, so it cannot go into a `static`/`thread_local`. That's exactly what the compiler is telling you.

Store the GIL-independent owned handle instead — `Py<PyArray1<i32>>` — which is `'static` and safe to keep around, then re-borrow it with a fresh `Python<'_>` token whenever you need to touch the data:

```rust
use pyo3::{Python, Py, Bound, pyfunction};
use numpy::PyArray1;
use std::cell::RefCell;

thread_local! {
    static ARRAYS: RefCell<Vec<Py<PyArray1<i32>>>> = RefCell::new(Vec::new());
}

#[pyfunction]
fn register_array(py: Python<'_>, x: Py<PyArray1<i32>>) {
    x.bind(py).readwrite().as_array_mut()[0] = 100;
    ARRAYS.with_borrow_mut(|v| v.push(x));
}
```

Later, call `arr.bind(py)` (or `.bind_borrow`) to get a usable array view again, always under a live `Python<'_>` token.
